{"maxId":0,"entries":{"asb-in-older-adults|ASB in Older Adults":["u82"],"uti-in-older-adults|UTI in Older Adults":["u94"],"risk-factors-in-older-adults-for-uti|Risk Factors in Older Adults for UTI":["u110"],"asymptomatic-bacteriuria|Asymptomatic Bacteriuria":["u131"],"presentation-and-evaluation-of-symptomatic-uti|Presentation and Evaluation of Symptomatic UTI":["u136"],"microbiology-of-uti|Microbiology of UTI":["u226"],"other-antimicrobial-sparing-agents|Other Antimicrobial Sparing Agents":["u389"],"prevention-of-catheter-associated-uti|Prevention of Catheter-Associated UTI":["u393"],"outcomes|Outcomes":["u417"],"antibiotic-resistance|Antibiotic Resistance":["u427"],"antimicrobial-stewardship-programs|Antimicrobial Stewardship Programs":["u451"],"copy|The prevalence of ASB increases with age and is more prevalent in those residing in PALTC settings.":["u82"],"copy|The incidence of UTI in postmenopausal women is 0.07 per person-year according to a large population-based study.":["u94"],"copy|Older adults may be at higher risk for development of UTI due to a variety of host factors associated with aging, such as a decline in B- and T-cell function.":["u110"],"copy|It has been well documented that clinicians should not screen older adults for ASB and those with ASB should not be treated with antibiotics.":["u131"],"copy|The presentation of UTI varies significantly in the older population and is particularly dependent on a person’s age, medical comorbidities (eg, diabetes, cerebrovascular disease, Parkinson disease, etc), structural or functional urinary tract abnormalities, demographics (ie, PALTC settings or community-dwelling), and presence of a urinary catheter (eg, urethral or suprapubic).":["u136"],"copy|The most frequent organism isolated from urinary cultures in both asymptomatic and symptomatic ambulatory elderly women is E coli, accounting for 75% to 95% of infections.":["u226"],"copy|In addition to the cranberry products and vaginal estrogens, several other agents have been used in clinical practice for prevention of UTI but their benefits remain uncertain.":["u389"],"copy|The most effective strategies for prevention of CAUTI are to avoid catheterization and, when an indwelling catheter is indicated, to discontinue the catheter as soon as possible.":["u393"],"copy|Most healthy older adults with symptomatic UTI respond well to antibiotic therapy.":["u417"],"copy|Antibiotic resistance is a large problem in all health care settings including outpatient, PALTC and acute hospital settings.":["u427"],"copy|UTIs are major driver of antibiotic use and inappropriate antibiotic prescribing related to UTI is common in various health care (including acute-care, outpatient, and PALTC) settings.":["u451"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[12,1],[15,-3],[16,0],[34,625],[37,-3],[38,431],[39,-4],[40,-3],[41,-5],[42,443],[43,447],[44,451],[45,454],[46,458],[47,462],[48,465],[49,475],[50,479],[51,483],[52,486],[53,490],[54,494],[55,498],[56,502],[57,506],[58,510],[59,514],[60,518],[61,521],[62,525],[63,529],[64,533],[65,537],[66,540],[67,543],[68,547],[69,551],[70,555],[71,559],[72,563],[73,589],[75,-37],[99,-36],[118,-35],[129,-34],[144,-33],[164,-32],[168,-31],[257,-30],[325,342],[327,-32],[399,265],[400,-32],[410,255],[411,-32],[421,-31],[424,-30],[447,-29],[456,-28],[479,-27],[496,170],[497,-27],[512,5],[519,130]]}
